or with me in New York  for many years a very very dear friend  you couldn't find a better secretary to  do that job the challenge there is the  integration of 22  you still have 22 agencies the synergies  that you need and the seamless as  particularly with respect to obtaining  information and then analyzing it  properly and acting on it it's a huge  challenge it would be the equivalent of  you know a big company taking over 22  small companies and anybody who's done  corporate work the integration the  execution of that is enormous ly  difficult and that's what's being done  and you know the people who are much  more knowledgeable about it who say this  this will work but we have to be patient  and in that process you know we're going  to overreact sometimes we're maybe going  to overreact to a threat and we simply  afraid to do that and worried about the  criticism I think the overall plan is a  good one I think we have to really work  hard to do that integration yes sir  first of all thank you very much for  your service secondly two questions one  is it correct that Osama bin Laden was  offered to the United States by Sudan in  the late 1990s and that offer was turned  down and secondly what do you expect to  come out from the CIA report regarding  able danger yeah no he was never offered  to the United States with respect to  able danger you know I don't know I am  intensely interested in that and as I  said I didn't agree with everything of  the 9/11 Commission said I am very  interested and I have a lot of questions  about you know at least what's been  reported in the media specifically that  you know two very very credible military  officers assigned to that unit and  apparently other people now who have  corroborated that story identified by  name and picture Mohamed Atta in 2000  has a al Qaeda operative in the United  States seems to me like that would be a  very relevant piece of any post 9/11  inquiry and I think it should be I don't  know what the r