th on the other side of that  yeah Danny  you're talking about they're not being  adequate resources allocated to the FBI  during your term has that changed if it  has changed and now are they adequate  now yes  well look everybody Washington complains  about resources and you know I certainly  was one of them and I felt that in  counterterrorism particularly you know  we didn't have what we needed to get the  job done and Congress was generous to us  we doubled the number of people work in  the program we triple the resources we  put together a counterterrorism  divisions the FBI we reorganized that we  didn't get anywhere near the resources  that we wanted since September 11th  there's been a great addition of  resources but there are only about 1400  more agents now than when I left office  and as I testified before the Joint  Intelligence Committee I don't think  that's sufficient 1,400 more agents to  focus on counterterrorism as we've made  it a priority but also I my day is to do  the very important work that only the  FBI does civil rights cases public  corruption cases we have examples of  that close by major organized crime case  and major white-collar crime cases now  what happened after September 11 we took  you know hundreds and hundreds of FBI  resources and put them in  counterterrorism but we took them out of  these other vital programs I don't think  that's smart as I testified before the  committee I said why don't we do  something really innovative and creative  give the FBI's 5,000 more agents and you  know 1,400 is great not enough to do all  the things that they do so well I need  to do again that's my opinion  yes ma'am  whereas  yeah as long as I'm not director I'm  okay with it that's right where we might  respect is better prepared to prevent a  9/11 one I think we're much better I  think we're much smarter I think we've  got much more capability overseas  domestically than we ever had before  we've got authorities like the Patriot  Act which I know people thought was  controversy but let me tell you another  example of something which I thought was  controversial if if before the Patriot  Act and before September 11th by the way  bin Laden was going to hold a rally in  the Sheep Meadow of Central Park and  announced his fought want an FBI agent  couldn't go and stand in the crowd it  was okay because there were guidelines  many many years old that prevented an  FBI agent from doing that and the  guidelines by the way we're actually put  in place to deal with some very serious  and illegal FBI conduct 25 years ago so  we're doing things a little bit better a  little bit smarter I think I think one  of the reasons you know we have not seen  the major attack in the United States  since September 11th is is that we've  gotten better at this we've exploited a  lot of overseas information and  witnesses that we never had access to  before  things that they got off hard drives in  Afghanistan and from the briefings of  prisoners and detainees have been  extremely valuable but you know this is  a very very vulnerable country as any  free country is and by the way as part  of that that we want to we want to  preserve but you know we've hardened up  federal buildings airports it took us  about an hour to get out of Philadelphia  last night you know and we all accept  that it's necessary but you know my  question is when I see I see a truck  delivering boxes to the vendors on the  other side of the security thing and I  wonder whose check  mmm and nobody'd ever really give me a  good explanation that our seaports and  the problem with someone who's willing  to die to attack us you've got you've  got you've got soccer stadiums you've  got malls you've got schools I mean you  saw what happened to the country or the  Capital Region a couple years ago with  with the snipers I mean it it almost  shut the area down if people were  holding their kids home from school they  were hiding behind their cars and  they're getting gas the balls were  deserted and that was two guys with a  mail-order rifle shooting people  randomly out of the back of a car so  we're very susceptible we're very prone  to that on the other hand you look at  the the courage of people whether it's  you know 9/11 in Washington or  Pennsylvania or New York and you know  we're very resilient and I think we're  doing things better  one more question yeah um during your  tenure as a director you spent a lot of  time outside of Washington visiting  field offices talking to agents and and  you've become a very popular director of  the FBI you how do you look at that  experience is that the way you want to  be looked at your legacy to that job um  you know one of the things one of the  things I thought was most important as  director was to you know to try to be as  available and accessible as I as you  could be yeah I mean I worked very hard  at that I did I went out all the time  through our offices I visit each of our  offices about three or four times most  of it was just for mental health getting  out of Washington DC by the way but the  more important thing was to find out you  know what people were doing and I would  go to the office I would meet with every  squad I wouldn't have allowed the essays  the guys in charge of the office  couldn't come to the meeting they didn't  like this at first and I'd have a  briefing book and I'd sit with the  agents and the support people and I'd  ask them questions how are we doing you  know we had a wonderful policy we just  enunciated in Washington we think it's  brilliant and then some guy would say it  stinks laws you know it's not working  never get that feedback by the way in  Washington you know you get very  elaborate briefings with graphics and  you know they have answers to all your  questions but you don't really know  what's going on in the field unless you  get out so that was very important the  other part of it was fun  it was one of the most fun things I did  to get out and see agents spend time  with them and you know as I said if I  said recently in this book I could be  more proud of than one of the FBI I I  can't tell you how extraordinary they  are in the circumstances you know under  which they work the stress the  sacrifices they and their families make  they are really really heroes for us and  part of the fun of this is getting a  chance to see some of them and getting  to thank them again  [Applause]
 

